       <details class="secondary-content"><summary>Try the cell-subdivision example</summary><section class="subdivision-panel" aria-labelledby="subdivision-title"><div class="learning-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">INTERACTIVE ILLUSTRATION / NO ENGINE RUN</p><h3 id="subdivision-title">More cells do not create more area.</h3></div><button id="subdivision-reset">Reset example</button></div><div class="subdivision-layout"><div><div id="subdivision-square" class="subdivision-square" role="group" aria-label="Illustrative parent cell"></div><p class="small muted">One illustrative parent, shown at a fixed size.</p></div><div><p>Start with one unresolved parent. Divide it into four equal children, then mark individual children accepted to see how counts and area change.</p><button id="subdivision-split">Subdivide into four children</button><div id="subdivision-readout" class="subdivision-readout" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div><p class="small muted">This is a teaching example, not a prediction of an engine result. Clicking a child changes only this example. The research snapshots stay fixed.</p></div></div></section></details>
       <details class="secondary-content"><summary>How to read this view</summary><div class="lesson-notes" data-disclosed="true"><article><span class="note-number">01</span><h3>Read a saved energy</h3><p>A marker records an approximate energy at one coordinate. A gap is the difference between two energies at that same point. Empty space between markers contains no sampled values.</p><button class="text-button" data-next="bands">Explore the 222 locations →</button></article><article><span class="note-number">02</span><h3>Read an accepted cell</h3><p>A cell is a region, not a point. The retained interval calculation checks its local external-band isolation condition over the whole cell. The cell’s area depends on its depth.</p><button class="text-button" data-next="coverage">Inspect the cell evidence →</button></article><article><span class="note-number">03</span><h3>Read the boundary of a claim</h3><p>Joining local results into a full-domain statement requires all relevant conditions to be met. The current partition retains unresolved regions, and its fixed cutoff does not prove convergence to an infinite model.</p></article></div></details>
       <details class="secondary-content"><summary>What changed when the model grew?</summary><p>Finer sampling in larger finite models puts the sampled narrow gap on one line near <strong>1.6 µeV</strong>, with cutoffs b and c agreeing to within 0.026 µeV. Separate local patches near two candidate touchings show <strong>b, c and d agreeing to within 0.03 µeV</strong> at the same sampled coordinates. Cutoff d was tested on those patches, not on the earlier line.</p><p>These are sampled values, not a gap-closure or global-minimum claim. The comparisons use central band indices in each finite model; close gap agreement does not identify individual states near a degeneracy or establish infinite-cutoff convergence.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/alanfuller15/Twistronics/pull/2#issuecomment-5843138081">CUTOFF-LADDER-003 · independent PASS</a> · <a href="https://github.com/alanfuller15/Twistronics/pull/2#issuecomment-5843549264">CUTOFF-SHELL-012 · independent PASS</a></p></details>
-      <details class="secondary-content" id="candidate-touchings"><summary>Candidate touching points: what the samples suggest</summary><p>Finite-cutoff numerical evidence is consistent with candidate external touchings near <strong>(0.6868, 0.7204)</strong> and <strong>(0.3596, 0.0185)</strong>, with negative discrete loop signs. These are approximate fractional momentum coordinates.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Previously reviewed loop evidence at the two candidate locations.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Candidate</th><th scope="col">Negative sign at cutoffs</th><th scope="col">Independent numerical review</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">R3 · (0.6868, 0.7204)</th><td>b, c</td><td><a href="https://github.com/alanfuller15/Twistronics/pull/2#issuecomment-5843418742">NODE-WINDING-006</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">R1 · (0.3596, 0.0185)</th><td>a, b, c</td><td><a href="https://github.com/alanfuller15/Twistronics/pull/2#issuecomment-5843536759">PARTNER-WINDING-011</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>At the first candidate (R3), the negative sign persists when the loop resolution increases from 16 to 32 to 64 points and when its radius is halved. A translated control loop has a positive sign. These robustness checks apply to R3 only. <a href="https://github.com/alanfuller15/Twistronics/pull/2#issuecomment-5843439638">LOOP-ROBUSTNESS-007 · independent PASS</a>.</p><p>A discrete loop sign is calculated from saved states around sampled points. This evidence does not certify a touching, node count, charge, partner correspondence, continuous isolation or infinite-cutoff convergence. The numerical reviews of 006 and 011 requested narrower interpretation; this panel uses their accepted wording.</p></details>
